1
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:14,981
Becky Burr, who was formally with NTIA, now an ICANN Board member.

2
00:00:14,981 --> 00:00:17,984
Becky, thank you for taking the time to talk to us.

3
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:22,989
If Vint Cerf is considered the father of the Internet, I’ve heard you refer to as the mother of the Internet,

4
00:00:22,989 --> 00:00:30,997
what was it like in those early years working with Ira Magaziner to get ICANN going?

5
00:00:30,997 --> 00:00:35,001
So, first of all, I have been called the godmother of ICANN,

6
00:00:35,001 --> 00:00:38,004
but I’m not going to take credit for the Internet.

7
00:00:38,004 --> 00:00:45,979
The early years were very exciting in the following way.

8
00:00:45,979 --> 00:00:58,992
There was a problem, funding for the IANA functions had been stopped by the National Science Foundation.

9
00:00:58,992 --> 00:01:08,001
There was need to sort of secure a firm foundation on which to continue the DNS work.

10
00:01:08,001 --> 00:01:17,977
There was, you know, talk about moving it into a more international organization kind of UN agency.

11
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:19,979
And a lot of people were very concerned about it.

12
00:01:19,979 --> 00:01:24,984
And a lot of people were very concerned about it from many, many different focuses.

13
00:01:24,984 --> 00:01:32,992
From keeping the Internet being able to continue to move at Internet speed,

14
00:01:32,992 --> 00:01:35,995
not having a heavy governmental overlay,

15
00:01:35,995 --> 00:01:42,001
allowing the sort of creativity that comes from private sector, or at large management.

16
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:48,007
And so, in order to figure out what to do,

17
00:01:48,007 --> 00:01:57,984
we conducted a very extensive sort of talking couple of months, where dozens and dozens, hundreds of people,

18
00:01:57,984 --> 00:02:05,992
came in and talked to us about what it was they needed to see going forward,

19
00:02:05,992 --> 00:02:09,996
what their problems with the way things were running then.

20
00:02:09,996 --> 00:02:14,000
There was a lot of disputes, trademark disputes,

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,005
other kinds of disputes that had been kind of building up.

22
00:02:19,005 --> 00:02:27,981
And so we got an enormous amount of input from speaking with people, and this was around the world.

23
00:02:27,981 --> 00:02:29,983
So, it wasn’t just the U.S.

24
00:02:29,983 --> 00:02:33,987
And then we sort of crafted the Green Paper,

25
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:37,991
which we wanted to throw out there and see what’s coming back at us.

26
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:44,998
We sort of put it out there, and we got thousands of comments,

27
00:02:44,998 --> 00:02:49,002
and thousands of comments, they were online comments, from around the world,

28
00:02:49,002 --> 00:02:56,976
which was, I think, in, this was 1997, it was probably a first in terms of online comments

29
00:02:56,976 --> 00:02:59,979
globally from the U.S. government perspective.

30
00:02:59,979 --> 00:03:07,987
So, then we did more talking, more talking around the world, more talking with people and produced the White Paper.

31
00:03:07,987 --> 00:03:10,990
There was a huge amount of excitement about it.

32
00:03:10,990 --> 00:03:14,994
There was an enormous amount of energy.

33
00:03:14,994 --> 00:03:16,996
Was there trepidation as well?

34
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:24,003
Well, of course there were some people that were saying that it wasn’t going to work,

35
00:03:24,003 --> 00:03:28,007
or that it should be managed by the ITU.

36
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:30,977
But in fact, it was a pretty small number.

37
00:03:30,977 --> 00:03:42,989
There was very strong global consensus for allowing this to move into a non-governmental setting.

38
00:03:42,989 --> 00:03:48,995
And in fact, it was really trying to replicate, or preserve, as much of what had been going on,

39
00:03:48,995 --> 00:03:52,999
because while DARPA had been funding the work that Jon [Postel] did,

40
00:03:52,999 --> 00:03:57,003
it was with a very light touch, and he was really running it.

41
00:03:57,003 --> 00:04:02,008
So, there was…  It sounds like there was pretty much universal acceptance that the DNS,

42
00:04:02,008 --> 00:04:08,982
management of the DNS, would at least go toward a private model

43
00:04:08,982 --> 00:04:10,984
instead of a government model.  Right?

44
00:04:10,984 --> 00:04:12,485
Yes. Yes, there was.

45
00:04:12,485 --> 00:04:18,992
And just to put this in context, that was a time in which there was enormous amount

46
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:23,997
of optimism and awareness about what the Internet was doing.

47
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:31,004
The U.S. government produced a paper on a framework for global Internet commerce,

48
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:34,007
that called for a very light regulatory touch.

49
00:04:34,007 --> 00:04:38,978
The European Union produced one.  There was one produced by Japan,

50
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:45,985
and there were big international conferences where people were very focused on

51
00:04:45,985 --> 00:04:51,991
not imposing a heavy governmental regulatory regime on the Internet.

52
00:04:51,991 --> 00:04:54,994
And there was that little bit of a sense of, you know, what if everyone did?

53
00:04:54,994 --> 00:04:58,998
So, you know, if you pass a regulation.

54
00:04:58,998 --> 00:05:08,007
So, ICANN grew up in the midst of a very vibrant global discussion about

55
00:05:08,007 --> 00:05:14,981
allowing the Internet to be the Internet, and a sort of light-handed regulatory approach to it.

56
00:05:14,981 --> 00:05:22,989
So, you know, some of the things that we have seen in more recent years, were really not there.

57
00:05:22,989 --> 00:05:30,997
The whole concept, ICANN is such an unusual beast in this sort of bottom-up policy formation system,

58
00:05:30,997 --> 00:05:37,003
with multiple inputs, unlike traditional organizations where it goes from the top-down.

59
00:05:37,003 --> 00:05:38,004
Where did that come from?

60
00:05:38,004 --> 00:05:43,009
It came from the…

61
00:05:43,009 --> 00:05:49,982
What we were saying was, the whole notion all along was private sector leadership.

62
00:05:49,982 --> 00:05:55,988
Now, today we wouldn’t call that, we wouldn’t say private sector leadership, we would say multistakeholder.

63
00:05:55,988 --> 00:05:58,991
We didn’t mean just business or anything like that,

64
00:05:58,991 --> 00:06:04,997
but stakeholders who were a part of it would be involved.

65
00:06:04,997 --> 00:06:08,000
That had always been the case.

66
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,006
Jon Postel worked very closely with the ITF and with the ccNSO managers and the like.

67
00:06:14,006 --> 00:06:19,979
There was a need in that transition to expand the definition of stakeholder.

68
00:06:19,979 --> 00:06:25,985
There were more stakeholders. There were end users. There were business users of the Internet.

69
00:06:25,985 --> 00:06:30,990
The pot was getting bigger. The stakeholder group was getting bigger.

70
00:06:30,990 --> 00:06:35,995
But what we wanted to do was really preserve what was going on

71
00:06:35,995 --> 00:06:42,001
and what had traditionally been the basis of DNS management,

72
00:06:42,001 --> 00:06:46,005
which is sort of consensus among the stakeholders,

73
00:06:46,005 --> 00:06:50,977
but recognize that the group of stakeholders had gotten much bigger,

74
00:06:50,977 --> 00:06:52,979
and much more diverse, and much more global.

75
00:06:52,979 --> 00:06:55,982
Help me understand the timeframe. Were you at NTIA then?

76
00:06:55,982 --> 00:07:00,987
I moved to NTIA on June 1st of 1997,

77
00:07:00,987 --> 00:07:09,996
and we published the first request for comment on June 2nd, or something like that.

78
00:07:09,996 --> 00:07:11,998
What was it like working with Magaziner on this endeavor?

79
00:07:11,998 --> 00:07:20,006
So, Ira was great. He had a group of people from all over the U.S. government

80
00:07:20,006 --> 00:07:23,976
working on the framework for Global Electronic Commerce.

81
00:07:23,976 --> 00:07:27,980
And I was working on privacy and Internet governance.

82
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:34,987
There were people from the U.S. trade rep, talking about tariffs.

83
00:07:34,987 --> 00:07:38,991
There were people from all over the government, putting together the sort of…

84
00:07:38,991 --> 00:07:50,002
If we want to enable us, to take advantage of the opportunity

85
00:07:50,002 --> 00:07:54,006
that the Internet represented from an economic growth perspective,

86
00:07:54,006 --> 00:07:57,009
what structure would we need to have in place.

87
00:07:57,009 --> 00:08:05,985
And so, this DNS management piece came directly out of the e-commerce.

88
00:08:05,985 --> 00:08:09,989
To that point, Magaziner told us, he said, you know,

89
00:08:09,989 --> 00:08:15,995
when he finished with the failed Clinton health care initiative, and he was going into this,

90
00:08:15,995 --> 00:08:21,000
he said a primary driver of this was we wanted a long-term economic growth model.

91
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:28,007
Was the economic viability of the Internet recognized by everybody at those very early stages?

92
00:08:28,007 --> 00:08:36,983
We were talking about what percentage of the global economy was already the,

93
00:08:36,983 --> 00:08:41,988
sort of, riding on the Internet, and how that would grow over time.

94
00:08:41,988 --> 00:08:50,997
So, there was a clear recognition that the Internet was the growth engine of the future,

95
00:08:50,997 --> 00:08:53,499
and that we needed to get it right.

96
00:08:53,499 --> 00:08:54,500
People got it.

97
00:08:55,001 --> 00:08:57,003
Yeah.  People definitely got it.

98
00:08:57,003 --> 00:09:01,007
Now, some…  I think actually, our numbers were probably quite low

99
00:09:01,007 --> 00:09:06,979
in terms of what we thought would be happening five years down the road.

100
00:09:06,979 --> 00:09:17,990
But globally, people understood that this was a paradigm shift like the Industrial Revolution in a way.

101
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:21,994
Was there across the board acceptance in,

102
00:09:21,994 --> 00:09:24,997
throughout the U.S. government, in the other agencies?

103
00:09:24,997 --> 00:09:31,003
Or was there… Did you have to argue this sort of, for lack of a better phrase,

104
00:09:31,003 --> 00:09:34,006
we built it, we sort of own it, attitude?

105
00:09:34,006 --> 00:09:38,978
There was remarkably little of that in the administration.

106
00:09:38,978 --> 00:09:44,984
Certainly the science agencies that had been part of it never had that attitude.

107
00:09:44,984 --> 00:09:52,992
I mean, they had gone global, although in a, you know, university and research institute model.

108
00:09:52,992 --> 00:10:01,000
Interestingly enough, there was a little more resistance to sort of commercialization,

109
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:11,978
but you know, you heard it once in a while, but not at all the executive order that President Clinton signed

110
00:10:11,978 --> 00:10:18,985
included moving this into... out of the government and the private sector.

111
00:10:18,985 --> 00:10:21,988
So, you know, every once in a while, you’d hear it, but.

112
00:10:21,988 --> 00:10:24,991
How about Capitol Hill, was there acceptance there?

113
00:10:24,991 --> 00:10:35,001
Well, there was. First of all, it’s pretty complex and what we were proposing to do was pretty unique.

114
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:43,976
We couldn’t point precisely to a model of what we were doing, because we were building something.

115
00:10:43,976 --> 00:10:49,982
Some people on the Hill understood that.

116
00:10:49,982 --> 00:10:58,991
There were, of course, some economic issues that created some lobbying on Capitol Hill.

117
00:10:58,991 --> 00:11:06,999
We were changing the competitive landscape in a pretty fundamental way.

118
00:11:06,999 --> 00:11:15,007
And the current winners in the economic landscape of the DNS

119
00:11:15,007 --> 00:11:20,980
were resistant a little bit, and they had some, got some traction on Capitol Hill.

120
00:11:20,980 --> 00:11:25,985
So, we had, you know, a series of hearings, and a series of investigations,

121
00:11:25,985 --> 00:11:28,988
to make sure that we were doing it right.

122
00:11:28,988 --> 00:11:36,996
What was the reaction…?  Throughout ICANN’s history, there have always been Congressional hearings about some aspect,

123
00:11:36,996 --> 00:11:42,001
whether it was the New gTLD Program, whether it was the IANA stewardship transition.

124
00:11:42,001 --> 00:11:50,009
What was the reaction both within the administration, multiple administrations, I guess,

125
00:11:50,009 --> 00:11:56,982
and globally for ICANN being called up to the legislative branch and being asked to explain this or that?

126
00:11:56,982 --> 00:12:06,992
Well, so frankly, I think, it’s not clear but for the change in VeriSign’s position in this.

127
00:12:06,992 --> 00:12:11,997
That it’s not clear to me how much Capitol Hill interests there would have been.

128
00:12:11,997 --> 00:12:13,999
I think that, honestly, generated the…

129
00:12:13,999 --> 00:12:15,501
You think that drove it?

130
00:12:15,501 --> 00:12:24,977
I think that there were… I know that there were concerted efforts to get Congress interested in this.

131
00:12:24,977 --> 00:12:27,980
Now, that’s fair game. I just want to make sure… People do it all of the time.

132
00:12:27,980 --> 00:12:29,482
But I want to make sure I understand what you’re saying.

133
00:12:29,482 --> 00:12:31,484
So, you’re saying VeriSign drove that interest.

134
00:12:31,484 --> 00:12:35,488
Yeah. And that happens all the time.

135
00:12:35,488 --> 00:12:40,493
And you would expect, you know, a publicly traded company,

136
00:12:40,493 --> 00:12:42,995
that has an obligation to maximize shareholder value,

137
00:12:42,995 --> 00:12:50,002
would take the steps that it could to retain its position.

138
00:12:50,002 --> 00:12:55,007
Had there not been that lobbying push, there would not have been as great an interest as there was?

139
00:12:55,007 --> 00:12:57,009
There would not have been as great an interest.

140
00:12:57,009 --> 00:13:01,981
There clearly would have been interest from the science committees.

141
00:13:01,981 --> 00:13:05,985
You know, the commerce committee would have looked at it,

142
00:13:05,985 --> 00:13:15,995
but we also had the judiciary committees looking at it from a trademark, an IP perspective.

143
00:13:15,995 --> 00:13:21,000
So, there were enough issues going on that I think that there would have been some hearings.

144
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,004
I suspect that they would not have been quite as hostile,

145
00:13:25,004 --> 00:13:32,978
but we did have a Democratic administration, and a Republican Congress at that time,

146
00:13:32,978 --> 00:13:38,984
so there would have been some of the natural partisan politics.

147
00:13:38,984 --> 00:13:45,991
Whenever ICANN was called up to the Hill, it always appeared to me that it was never truly understood.

148
00:13:45,991 --> 00:13:49,995
That whenever there was a hearing, even right up to the end,

149
00:13:49,995 --> 00:13:52,998
when the final Cruz hearing on the transition.

150
00:13:52,998 --> 00:13:58,003
There was a sort of underlying current that a lot of members just didn’t quite get ICANN.

151
00:13:58,003 --> 00:14:04,009
Well, I think that’s right.  They didn’t get the DNS, right?

152
00:14:04,009 --> 00:14:07,980
So, you have to understand the DNS to begin with,

153
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:13,986
and then you have to understand how the management is dispersed,

154
00:14:13,986 --> 00:14:21,994
and how there really isn’t a kind of central, other than the coordination role that ICANN plays.

155
00:14:21,994 --> 00:14:25,998
It’s not a single monolithic… It’s not a thing.

156
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:36,008
It’s a, you know, vast network of networks managed by the DNS system, or navigated by the DNS system.

157
00:14:36,008 --> 00:14:42,982
It’s complicated. We’ve gotten better about explaining it.

158
00:14:42,982 --> 00:14:47,987
The other thing is that, to the extent there was a "we built it, we own it"

159
00:14:47,987 --> 00:14:56,996
that was based on this fundamental misunderstanding about what it meant to be the authoritative root, right?

160
00:14:56,996 --> 00:15:06,005
And Jon tried to demonstrate that one night, by directing the root server system to point to another authoritative root.

161
00:15:06,005 --> 00:15:10,976
That could have been, for those of us who understood what he was doing,

162
00:15:10,976 --> 00:15:16,982
it was clearly Jon saying, "you guys are just confused. Here is how it works."

163
00:15:16,982 --> 00:15:21,987
It wasn’t Jon pushing back about the government, it was Jon trying to…

164
00:15:21,987 --> 00:15:22,988
Well…

165
00:15:22,988 --> 00:15:23,989
I’ve heard both.

166
00:15:23,989 --> 00:15:32,998
No, I do not… I think it was a moment in which Jon was trying to demonstrate

167
00:15:32,998 --> 00:15:37,002
that some of the arguments were quite silly.

168
00:15:37,002 --> 00:15:48,981
And taking the opportunity to, in a very tangible way, demonstrate where control lay

169
00:15:48,981 --> 00:15:53,986
and how control could move from place to place.

170
00:15:53,986 --> 00:16:00,993
Now, that’s the way I always interpret it.

171
00:16:00,993 --> 00:16:08,000
There were multiple administrations, the Bush administration, the Clinton administration, and so on.

172
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:17,977
Could you ascertain a difference in the way that ICANN was handled or dealt with,

173
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:20,980
from administration to administration?  Was there a difference?

174
00:16:20,980 --> 00:16:33,993
So, you know, the Clinton administration ended in 2000, and it was still really in its infancy.

175
00:16:33,993 --> 00:16:42,001
We clearly had the commitment to complete the transition and move it forward.

176
00:16:42,001 --> 00:16:52,978
I think that that could have been... that might have been the way subsequent administrations would have proceeded.

177
00:16:52,978 --> 00:17:00,986
It took ICANN a while to get sort of stable, and we had the 2003, 2004 evolution and reform committee,

178
00:17:00,986 --> 00:17:03,989
but what really changed things was September 11th.

179
00:17:03,989 --> 00:17:18,003
At that moment, there was a sort of, where are all of the potential control, safety kinds of places.

180
00:17:18,003 --> 00:17:24,009
And I think that for a while the intelligence community was pretty convinced

181
00:17:24,009 --> 00:17:30,983
that there was an important reason to control the authoritative root.

182
00:17:30,983 --> 00:17:36,989
That changed by the end of the Bush administration.

183
00:17:36,989 --> 00:17:41,994
And they had sort of worked through the evaluation

184
00:17:41,994 --> 00:17:46,999
and understood the system had gotten much more robust, and much more dispersed, and all of those things.

185
00:17:46,999 --> 00:17:53,005
And so, while there was always this commitment to transfer,

186
00:17:53,005 --> 00:18:00,979
ultimately it happened that there was a period of time where there was increasing anxiety

187
00:18:00,979 --> 00:18:06,985
about what would happen if we didn’t have this sort of back stop control over the root,

188
00:18:06,985 --> 00:18:14,993
and ICANN was... It was hard work to sort of get to a stable period.

189
00:18:14,993 --> 00:18:19,998
So, that kind of supported allowing this time to pass.

190
00:18:19,998 --> 00:18:24,002
But by the end of the Bush administration, it was pretty clear that

191
00:18:24,002 --> 00:18:34,980
people had understood that this wasn’t a sort of national security tool.

192
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:42,988
And so that paved the way in the Obama administration for finishing the transition.

193
00:18:42,988 --> 00:18:46,992
Let me ask you about another issue, and you were involved in this one, which was .xxx.

194
00:18:46,992 --> 00:18:52,998
Was this a case…?  Because it’s often pointed to as this is a case where the government did try to influence ICANN.

195
00:18:52,998 --> 00:19:04,977
So, we did do FOIAs and we... I don’t think that there is anything in the written record

196
00:19:04,977 --> 00:19:10,983
that proves that the U.S. government intervened.

197
00:19:10,983 --> 00:19:19,992
I had conversations with people who were directly in that line.

198
00:19:19,992 --> 00:19:23,996
The U.S. government did intervene in the .xxx decision.

199
00:19:23,996 --> 00:19:31,003
And it was sort of after the fact, because we had been working very closely with NTIA

200
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:36,008
to make sure they understood what the TLD was about, what was going on.

201
00:19:36,008 --> 00:19:41,980
It was all fine until after ICANN Board had actually already approved it,

202
00:19:41,980 --> 00:19:49,988
and at that moment, there was a political, a very strong political pushback,

203
00:19:49,988 --> 00:19:56,995
somebody reaching, conservative organizations reaching back into the Bush administration at the highest level,

204
00:19:56,995 --> 00:20:00,999
which resulted in really a sea change, from NTIA’s perspective.

205
00:20:00,999 --> 00:20:04,002
How would you characterize…?  I asked Vint Cerf this question.

206
00:20:04,002 --> 00:20:10,008
I said, how would you characterize, historically, the USG’s relationship with ICANN?

207
00:20:10,008 --> 00:20:14,980
And he said problematic and not very supportive.  How would you characterize it?

208
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:23,989
Well, I think that there was support initially.

209
00:20:23,989 --> 00:20:33,999
There was a great deal of frustration on the part of NTIA in the early years.

210
00:20:33,999 --> 00:20:41,006
The IANA functions were not delivered in the best way. They were not…

211
00:20:41,006 --> 00:20:44,009
There were delays, it took a long time.

212
00:20:44,009 --> 00:20:47,980
They got lots and lots and lots of complaints from the direct consumers,

213
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:54,987
that just serving the IANA functions was difficult.

214
00:20:54,987 --> 00:21:02,995
There were some kind of heavy handed moves to try to get country code top level domains

215
00:21:02,995 --> 00:21:11,003
to enter into contracts that were well-intended, but seriously backfired.

216
00:21:11,003 --> 00:21:17,976
And so, there was a great deal of frustration in the early days

217
00:21:17,976 --> 00:21:22,981
with performance and sort of heavy handed tactics.

218
00:21:22,981 --> 00:21:26,985
And I think that the government pushed back on that.

219
00:21:26,985 --> 00:21:38,997
And then after that, until Larry Strickling sort of got fully up to speed about it,

220
00:21:38,997 --> 00:21:42,000
I would imagine it could have been difficult.

221
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:51,977
I think that Larry was fully supportive of ICANN throughout,

222
00:21:51,977 --> 00:21:57,983
but I think there was probably frustration on both sides, right up to the end.

223
00:21:57,983 --> 00:22:00,986
Did you think the transition was going to fly?

224
00:22:00,986 --> 00:22:10,996
I was really happy on October 1st, I just have to say. I think it was touch and go.

225
00:22:10,996 --> 00:22:17,002
But between the announcement in 2014, and when it finally happened in October of 2016,

226
00:22:17,002 --> 00:22:24,009
were there points where you’re going, "I don’t think this is going to work"?

227
00:22:24,009 --> 00:22:26,978
Well, I think that until the community…

228
00:22:26,978 --> 00:22:29,981
The community came together right after the announcement,

229
00:22:29,981 --> 00:22:36,988
in which the announcement was really focused on the IANA functions technical transition.

230
00:22:36,988 --> 00:22:45,997
And the community stood up and said, in a kind of astonishing unity, that never before seen, "that’s not good enough."

231
00:22:45,997 --> 00:22:50,001
"We need these accountability fixes. This is the moment to do this."

232
00:22:50,001 --> 00:22:58,009
And ICANN resisted.  And frankly, so did NTIA, didn't really understand why we needed this.

233
00:22:58,009 --> 00:23:11,990
So, we did both complicate the transition process by demanding a more robust review and change to get there.

234
00:23:11,990 --> 00:23:20,999
And I was very worried about it until ICANN finally acknowledged the community’s views on this,

235
00:23:20,999 --> 00:23:28,006
and worked to put together the CCWG, the Cross Community Working Group on Accountability.

236
00:23:28,006 --> 00:23:31,009
After that, I felt pretty good about it.

237
00:23:31,009 --> 00:23:36,481
There were, you know, moments in between now and then, and it was…

238
00:23:36,481 --> 00:23:40,986
It obviously took a lot longer than anybody had hoped that it would. But…

239
00:23:40,986 --> 00:23:46,992
You have had a unique perspective in that you were at NTIA, you were an attorney

240
00:23:46,992 --> 00:23:50,996
involved in the dot triple X case, and now you’re an ICANN Board member.

241
00:23:50,996 --> 00:23:53,999
So, you’ve been along the entire continuum.

242
00:23:53,999 --> 00:23:58,003
Well so, actually, it’s a little more complicated than that.

243
00:23:58,003 --> 00:24:02,007
I was at NTIA, but I was also the GAC representative from the United States

244
00:24:02,007 --> 00:24:04,976
to the GAC for the first couple of years.

245
00:24:04,976 --> 00:24:06,978
And then, I was a lawyer in private practice,

246
00:24:06,978 --> 00:24:11,983
but I was also on the Country Code Name Supporting Organization Council for many years.

247
00:24:11,983 --> 00:24:16,988
So, I have had a kind of nice…

248
00:24:16,988 --> 00:24:22,994
So, my question, given that broad range of experience, how has that shaped your perception of ICANN?

249
00:24:22,994 --> 00:24:36,007
Well, I think I have a pretty good view of sort of what the drivers are across the organization.

250
00:24:36,007 --> 00:24:41,980
I think I understand and share the concerns, have a sort of…

251
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:46,985
I have many, many different perspectives. I can look at it and say,

252
00:24:46,985 --> 00:24:51,990
as a country code manager, you know, here are the issues at stake.

253
00:24:51,990 --> 00:24:57,996
As a business participant, as an industry participant in the DNS industry, here are the concerns.

254
00:24:57,996 --> 00:25:00,999
As a government, here are the concerns.

255
00:25:00,999 --> 00:25:09,007
And as a, you know, member of the Board, here are the concerns, you put them together and figure it out.

256
00:25:09,007 --> 00:25:12,978
Great. Becky Burr, thank you very much. Appreciate you taking the time to talk to us.

257
00:25:12,978 --> 00:25:14,479
It’s a pleasure.